{ An interesting pawn sacrifice, as 11.exd5 Bxd5 12.bxc3 O-O gives us beautiful control over the light squares as well as a pretty bishop sitting on d5. }
11... dxe4 12. Sd2 Dc7
{ ;5.Bg5 Nf6 6.Nc3 Be6 7.e3 #4-7) 5.Bg5 Lifetime Repertoires: Tarrasch Defense (11.31) Just in time defending all the pawns. Imagine if we would have played 9...O-O instead of 9...Nbd7, we would be forced to meet 13.Bxf6 by 13...gxf6. A subtle, but crucial difference. }
) 10... O-O 11. Sxd7
{ Seemingly the most tempting choice, ruining our pawn structure on the kingside for good. }
( 11. Lf3
{ If White does not wish to part with their dark-squared bishop this would be the logical alternative. Mounting the pressure on d5 to the absolute maximum. }
11... Da5
{ Getting out of the pin just in time, while still keeping an eye on d5. } 12.
Sxd7
{ White does best exchanging the knight, as otherwise, we may entertain thoughts of playing ...Nxe5 ourselves. }
12... Sxd7 13. Dc2
{ The right choice by White. Slightly improving the queen as well as making sure ...Bf5 is not played soon. }
13... h6
{ There are several other possibilities, but I like this useful move, as well as gaining a tempo. It is never a bad idea to create some air for the king in the event of an unlikely back-rank checkmate later. }
14. Lh4 Tfe8
{ ;5.Bg5 Nf6 6.Nc3 Be6 7.e3 #5-7) 5.Bg5 Lifetime Repertoires: Tarrasch Defense (11.32) Another small move, just improving the rook a bit. The game has reached the phase of a slow, yet highly complex middlegame with chances for both sides. We should attempt to improve our knight next, which can be done via two routes. Either via b6 to c8 and d6, or possibly even to a4 after an eventual exchange on c3. Another viable manoeuvre would be via f8 to g6 bothering the white bishop. Neither should we forget about playing ...b5 at an opportune moment. }
) ( 11. f4
{ An overly aggressive push, as there will be no real attack and it only leaves the e4-square permanently weak. }
11... Lxc3 { Eliminating the knight, so that d5 is no longer attacked. } 12.
bxc3 Lf5
{ ;5.Bg5 Nf6 6.Nc3 Be6 7.e3 #6-7) 5.Bg5 Lifetime Repertoires: Tarrasch Defense (11.33) Just in time, stopping f5. We will be putting the newly acquired square on e4 to good use by either our bishop or the knight. }
) 11... Dxd7
{ The only right recapture, as 11...Bxd7 would run into 12.Nxd5! . } 12. Lxf6
gxf6
{ Thus, our pawn structure on the kingside is not the prettiest in the history of the game of chess. But by now this should be a familiar sight and we should be aware of the advantages our position has as well. The bishop pair in combination with our majority over on the queenside will generate good counterplay. Moreover, our doubled pawns are no easy target whatsoever, and we can easily make them a strength playing ...f5 and stopping e3-e4 forever. }
13. Lf3 Kh8
{ There were a wealth of different options at our disposal, but I quite like this move. Tucking away our king is always going to be beneficial, and who knows, in the distant future we might use the g-file for an attack. }
14. a3 Lxc3
{ It is good to be aware that if provoked by a2-a3 this often is a good exchange for us. Even though we lose the bishop pair, there is no more pressure on d5, and we get to improve our light-squared bishop. }
15. bxc3 Lf5
{ ;5.Bg5 #3-Quickstarter Guide Lifetime Repertoires: Tarrasch Defense (3.19) Our bishop stands excellently here and by now, we are ready to advance on the queenside. If needed, we can stop e3-e4 by ...Rfe8. }
